/*! \page page_network AnCH network library documentation
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section network_intro_seq Introduction
 *
 * AnCH network library provides TCP and UDP sockets, IPv4 and IPv6, with C++ streams for TCP.
 *
 * \section network_prereq_sec Prerequisites
 *
 * anch-cutils: streams over sockets.
 *
 * \section network_install_sec Installation
 *
 * CMake target \c anch::network:
 * \code{.cmake}
find_package(Anch REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my-app PRIVATE anch::network)
\endcode
 * See \ref install_sec "installation".
 *
 * \section network_concept_sec Usage
 *
 * - \ref anch::network::TcpStream "TcpStream" is a connected TCP socket and a \c std::iostream : use
 *   \c << , \c >> , \c std::getline , ... \c shutdown half-closes the connection (end of transmission).
 * - \ref anch::network::TcpServer "TcpServer" listens on an address (port \c 0 for a free port) and accepts
 *   connections as \ref anch::network::TcpStream "TcpStream".
 * - \ref anch::network::UdpSocket "UdpSocket" sends and receives datagrams.
 * - \ref anch::network::Socket "Socket" is the RAII socket descriptor ; errors are
 *   \ref anch::network::IOException "IOException" (a \c std::system_error with \c errno ).
 *
 * \c SIGPIPE is never raised: writing on a closed connection raises an exception (or sets the stream bad bit).
 *
 * \section network_example_sec Example
 *
 * Echo server and client:
 * \code{.cpp}
#include "network/tcpServer.hpp"
#include "network/tcpStream.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <thread>

int
main() {
  anch::network::TcpServer server("127.0.0.1", 0);              // port 0: free port
  uint16_t port = server.getAddress().getPort();

  std::thread echo([&server]() {
    anch::network::TcpStream peer = server.accept();              // TcpStream is a std::iostream
    std::string line;
    while(std::getline(peer, line)) {
      peer << "echo: " << line << std::endl;
    }
  });

  anch::network::TcpStream client("127.0.0.1", port);
  client << "hello" << std::endl;
  std::string answer;
  std::getline(client, answer);
  std::cout << answer << std::endl;
  client.shutdown();                                              // end of transmission: server loop ends
  echo.join();
  return 0;
}
\endcode
 */
